WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.029 --> 00:00:10.940
In talking about, in talking about black holes,
of course.

00:00:10.940 --> 00:00:16.400
Stephen Hawking is the great scientist who
comes to mind, so I'd like to spend a little

00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:24.259
bit of time thinking about what it is that
Hawking told us about black holes.

00:00:24.259 --> 00:00:29.720
What were the insights that he gave us, and
what puzzles emerged from his work that we

00:00:29.720 --> 00:00:35.800
are still struggling to resolve and probably
will continue to struggle with for some time?

00:00:35.800 --> 00:00:42.810
And toward that end, as a little bit of background,
we need to have some understanding of a concept

00:00:42.810 --> 00:00:48.050
that everybody is familiar with it at some
level or another, but let's use this opportunity

00:00:48.050 --> 00:00:50.470
to get us all in the same place.

00:00:50.470 --> 00:00:53.530
And that's a concept of entropy.

00:00:53.530 --> 00:00:59.069
So entropy is a word that's free, they used,
and the culture, we often use it to describe

00:00:59.069 --> 00:01:03.850
a certain amount of going from order to disorder,
that's going from low entropy to high entropy.

00:01:03.850 --> 00:01:07.880
That's sort of the intuition that the culture
has about this word.

00:01:07.880 --> 00:01:11.780
So let's start with that very basic way of
thinking about entropy.

00:01:11.780 --> 00:01:16.509
And a good example would be, let's say I take
my book, I throw it up in the air, it starts

00:01:16.509 --> 00:01:17.630
very ordered.

00:01:17.630 --> 00:01:23.810
All the pages were in complete, numerical
order, but as they come down, it's overwhelmingly

00:01:23.810 --> 00:01:27.430
likely that they will not land in numerical
order.

00:01:27.430 --> 00:01:32.649
They'll land in a highly disordered state
and that gives you some sense of what it means

00:01:32.649 --> 00:01:40.469
to go from an ordered arrangement to a highly
disordered arrangement, going from low entropy

00:01:40.469 --> 00:01:41.620
to higher entropy.

00:01:41.620 --> 00:01:45.399
Now, of course, that's just a sort of fanciful,
everyday example.

00:01:45.399 --> 00:01:51.520
If we head more toward a physics example,
we could imagine having a box of gas, and

00:01:51.520 --> 00:01:57.810
imagine it has a small number of atoms in
there, and imagine that they're all neatly

00:01:57.810 --> 00:02:01.310
arranged in this nice cubic lattice.

00:02:01.310 --> 00:02:08.360
This would be a very ordered or low entropy
configuration of the gas, like the pages all

00:02:08.360 --> 00:02:09.670
in numerical order.

00:02:09.670 --> 00:02:15.640
Now, contrast that with a box of gas that
has a whole lot of atoms, a whole lot of particles

00:02:15.640 --> 00:02:19.879
are all bouncing around in a completely haphazard
way.

00:02:19.879 --> 00:02:25.920
This would be a very disordered state, a high
entropy state.

00:02:25.920 --> 00:02:32.980
And another way of thinking about the distinction
between low entropy and high entropy, which

00:02:32.980 --> 00:02:38.590
we'll be making use of in just a moment is
the amount of information that would be required

00:02:38.590 --> 00:02:42.180
to describe these two configurations.

00:02:42.180 --> 00:02:44.709
Because look on the left, you don't need a
lot of data.

00:02:44.709 --> 00:02:45.709
It's a cubic lattice.

00:02:45.709 --> 00:02:50.100
You've got three by three by three atom filling
out that lattice.

00:02:50.100 --> 00:02:54.820
And if I gave you the separation of them,
say 10 centimeters, that basically describes

00:02:54.820 --> 00:02:56.340
the configuration on the left.

00:02:56.340 --> 00:03:01.180
You don't need a lot of information to describe
the system that has low entropy.

00:03:01.180 --> 00:03:04.709
But let's say I want to describe the configuration
on the right.

00:03:04.709 --> 00:03:09.200
Well, I basically have to tell you where each
and every particle is located.

00:03:09.200 --> 00:03:10.500
That's the only way I could describe it.

00:03:10.500 --> 00:03:14.620
Look at all the information I need to describe
that configuration.

00:03:14.620 --> 00:03:19.030
So the idea is low entropy, not a lot of information.

00:03:19.030 --> 00:03:23.900
High entropy takes a lot of information that's
hidden in this configuration of particles

00:03:23.900 --> 00:03:29.650
and that's kind of the distinction between
order, disorder, low entropy, high entropy,

00:03:29.650 --> 00:03:32.200
low information, high information.

00:03:32.200 --> 00:03:38.680
Now of course I'm talking in loose language
so that we can all get a feel for the critical

00:03:38.680 --> 00:03:43.010
ideas, but you can make this all quite precise.

00:03:43.010 --> 00:03:47.299
And this gentleman over here, you know who
this is?

00:03:47.299 --> 00:03:48.299
Who's this?

00:03:48.299 --> 00:03:49.299
Ludwig Boltzmann.

00:03:49.299 --> 00:03:50.299
That's right.

00:03:50.299 --> 00:03:56.680
And this is his, his famous tombstone, and
you see that formula on the top of his tombstone,

00:03:56.680 --> 00:04:00.079
and that is the formula that makes these statements
precise.

00:04:00.079 --> 00:04:05.050
This is a formula that you learn if you take
a course in statistical mechanics, thermodynamics.

00:04:05.050 --> 00:04:07.590
S stands for entropy.

00:04:07.590 --> 00:04:12.590
K is Boltzmann's constant, and the rest of
it has to do with the number of configuration

00:04:12.590 --> 00:04:16.130
of the ingredients of the particles that make
up the system.

00:04:16.130 --> 00:04:17.130
You.

00:04:17.130 --> 00:04:22.870
You got a job at Harvard as faculty, but that's
okay.

00:04:22.870 --> 00:04:23.990
We're still.

00:04:23.990 --> 00:04:28.410
We're still good-
You are doing very well too.

00:04:28.410 --> 00:04:29.410
Thank you.

00:04:29.410 --> 00:04:49.610
And I should say my most exciting intellectual
adventures in string theory have been the

00:04:49.610 --> 00:04:55.880
papers that we have done together.

00:04:55.880 --> 00:04:58.560
A lot of fun with you Brian.

00:04:58.560 --> 00:04:59.560
Thank you.

00:04:59.560 --> 00:05:01.420
So it's been an honor to work with you.

00:05:01.420 --> 00:05:02.420
So thank you.

00:05:02.420 --> 00:05:06.500
So, so let's, let's now get into this subject
here.

00:05:06.500 --> 00:05:13.840
We heard a little bit about entropy and about
the second law that entropy increases over

00:05:13.840 --> 00:05:20.810
time, but in the 1970s, people like Bekenstein
and, and Hawking, and they began to worry

00:05:20.810 --> 00:05:25.230
about how the second law of thermodynamics
would behave in the environment of a black

00:05:25.230 --> 00:05:26.230
hole.

00:05:26.230 --> 00:05:46.980
And give us a sense of what they were puzzling
about.

00:05:46.980 --> 00:05:48.970
You.

00:05:48.970 --> 00:05:51.750
You got a job at Harvard as faculty, but that's
okay.

00:05:51.750 --> 00:05:52.750
We're still.

00:05:52.750 --> 00:05:56.690
We're still good-
You are doing very well too.

00:05:56.690 --> 00:05:57.690
Thank you.

00:05:57.690 --> 00:06:06.630
And I should say my most exciting intellectual
adventures in string theory have been the

00:06:06.630 --> 00:06:08.849
papers that we have done together.

00:06:08.849 --> 00:06:10.009
A lot of fun with you Brian.

00:06:10.009 --> 00:06:11.009
Thank you.

00:06:11.009 --> 00:06:12.009
So it's been an honor to work with you.

00:06:12.009 --> 00:06:13.009
So thank you.

00:06:13.009 --> 00:06:17.020
So, so let's, let's now get into this subject
here.

00:06:17.020 --> 00:06:22.419
We heard a little bit about entropy and about
the second law that entropy increases over

00:06:22.419 --> 00:06:32.289
time, but in the 1970s, people like Bekenstein
and, and Hawking, and they began to worry

00:06:32.289 --> 00:06:37.660
about how the second law of thermodynamics
would behave in the environment of a black

00:06:37.660 --> 00:06:38.660
hole.

00:06:38.660 --> 00:06:40.540
And give us a sense of what they were puzzling
about.

00:06:40.540 --> 00:06:46.880
So part of the puzzle was the objects they
knew falls into the black holes, but they

00:06:46.880 --> 00:06:48.780
cannot come out.

00:06:48.780 --> 00:06:53.400
So objects that are falling in have entropy,
just like what Brian already described, you

00:06:53.400 --> 00:06:58.039
have some information about where they are,
how they are, but once you fall into a black

00:06:58.039 --> 00:07:00.680
hole then you don't have anymore access to
that information.

00:07:00.680 --> 00:07:03.479
So what happened to that information?

00:07:03.479 --> 00:07:05.389
So that seems like you lost entropy.

00:07:05.389 --> 00:07:08.050
Entropy went down and that's against what
Boltzmann said.

00:07:08.050 --> 00:07:10.330
So can you give a concrete example of that?

00:07:10.330 --> 00:07:13.340
So I think we have a little silly example.

00:07:13.340 --> 00:07:17.850
If I took my wallet, it has information, it
has entropy.

00:07:17.850 --> 00:07:22.889
I throw it into the black hole and you're
asking what happened to that entropy?

00:07:22.889 --> 00:07:24.300
Did it disappear from the universe?

00:07:24.300 --> 00:07:25.300
Exactly.

00:07:25.300 --> 00:07:28.020
So that was the kind of question that they
were struggling to understand.

00:07:28.020 --> 00:07:29.720
How could this be consistent?

00:07:29.720 --> 00:07:30.720
Right.

00:07:30.720 --> 00:07:35.430
So, uh, they began thinking about this and
then there were a few ideas that they were

00:07:35.430 --> 00:07:39.919
thinking about, and in particular the properties
of the horizon of a black hole-

00:07:39.919 --> 00:07:40.919
The edge.

00:07:40.919 --> 00:07:44.389
The edge, the event horizon that we thought-
The edge in which- beyond which you can- no

00:07:44.389 --> 00:07:46.039
light can escape.

00:07:46.039 --> 00:07:50.300
They found it had some interesting properties,
and in particular they began studying what

00:07:50.300 --> 00:07:55.000
is the area, and what is the area of this
edge, and how does it change.

00:07:55.000 --> 00:08:01.720
And in particular, what happens if you throw
objects into this black hole, what happens

00:08:01.720 --> 00:08:03.240
to this area?

00:08:03.240 --> 00:08:08.639
And one of the surprising things they found
was that when you throw objects in, this area

00:08:08.639 --> 00:08:11.479
cannot stay put, and in fact increases.

00:08:11.479 --> 00:08:12.599
Yep.

00:08:12.599 --> 00:08:17.259
So they began to think about, okay, what could
this mean about this- What, what this could

00:08:17.259 --> 00:08:22.900
be related in some way compensated by the
fact that you're losing something, the entropy.

00:08:22.900 --> 00:08:27.580
Could it be somehow compensated by the fact
that this area is growing?

00:08:27.580 --> 00:08:33.610
So they begin to ask the question, could the
area of this event horizon be in any way related

00:08:33.610 --> 00:08:35.460
to the lost entropy?

00:08:35.460 --> 00:08:37.200
So we actually can unpack that.

00:08:37.200 --> 00:08:41.150
So let's just go through a couple of little
examples here.

00:08:41.150 --> 00:08:43.340
So this is the standards.

00:08:43.340 --> 00:08:44.350
Second Law of thermodynamics.

00:08:44.350 --> 00:08:46.120
Entropy grows over time.

00:08:46.120 --> 00:08:49.960
Let's just take the two boxes that I started
with to illustrate what you're describing.

00:08:49.960 --> 00:08:54.650
If we take those two boxes and they put them
together, what happened to the entropy?

00:08:54.650 --> 00:08:55.650
It goes up.

00:08:55.650 --> 00:08:57.090
The Boltzmann's thing.

00:08:57.090 --> 00:08:58.090
Right.

00:08:58.090 --> 00:09:01.080
So we have the combined entropy is greater
than the individuals entropy.

00:09:01.080 --> 00:09:02.080
Great.

00:09:02.080 --> 00:09:05.840
Now you're describing something that doesn't
have to do with gas in a box.

00:09:05.840 --> 00:09:09.850
You're talking about the geometry of a black
hole.

00:09:09.850 --> 00:09:16.380
So for example, they studied what happens
if you study one black hole with one area

00:09:16.380 --> 00:09:21.340
of event horizon and another black hole with
a different area, and you bring them together,

00:09:21.340 --> 00:09:24.520
you merge them and you get a new black hole
with a new area.

00:09:24.520 --> 00:09:31.430
And they found that the area of the new formed
black hole is bigger than the sum of the two

00:09:31.430 --> 00:09:34.550
areas, just like the entropy had that property.

00:09:34.550 --> 00:09:39.760
The entropy resulting in the box when you
combined them was bigger than the individual

00:09:39.760 --> 00:09:40.760
entropies.

00:09:40.760 --> 00:09:46.580
So the entropy sounded very similar to the
area, or areas sounded very similar to the

00:09:46.580 --> 00:09:47.580
entropy.

00:09:47.580 --> 00:09:52.790
So they began asking, could it be that the
area and entropy are proportional to one another

00:09:52.790 --> 00:09:54.720
for black holes?

00:09:54.720 --> 00:09:59.180
And indeed they found a formula that we can
bring up here.

00:09:59.180 --> 00:10:06.890
So entropy is the area, and the proportionality
constant is a little bit complicated but you

00:10:06.890 --> 00:10:11.100
don't really have to worry too much about
that, this number on the right hand side,

00:10:11.100 --> 00:10:18.580
but the basic lesson is that they're guessing,
they're suggesting that the area of the event

00:10:18.580 --> 00:10:24.920
horizon is somehow holding on to the entropy,
holding onto the information in some sense

00:10:24.920 --> 00:10:26.980
that's inside the black hole.

00:10:26.980 --> 00:10:30.550
Now you gave an interesting thought experiment
about how to test that.

00:10:30.550 --> 00:10:35.850
You're talking about throwing something in
and watching the area, and it just so happens

00:10:35.850 --> 00:10:39.740
that we have a little segment on that here.

00:10:39.740 --> 00:10:41.400
So why don't we go through that.

00:10:41.400 --> 00:10:42.400
So here's a-.

00:10:42.400 --> 00:10:43.400
So take us.

00:10:43.400 --> 00:10:44.400
So what are we seeing here?

00:10:44.400 --> 00:10:45.400
And then we'll do your little thought experiment.

00:10:45.400 --> 00:10:47.331
So here I guess are seeing a black hole.

00:10:47.331 --> 00:10:49.410
The red black hole.

00:10:49.410 --> 00:10:51.839
Yes, exactly.

00:10:51.839 --> 00:10:57.030
With the event horizon of it, you can see
that the idea that the information is proportional

00:10:57.030 --> 00:11:00.080
to the area with some proportionality constant.

00:11:00.080 --> 00:11:04.250
If each individual element of the information
is like one square there, we just divided

00:11:04.250 --> 00:11:05.980
this sphere into these areas.

00:11:05.980 --> 00:11:11.260
So these represent bits of information on
that surface of that black hole.

00:11:11.260 --> 00:11:16.270
So those little squares can roughly speaking,
be thought of as Planck areas.

00:11:16.270 --> 00:11:20.120
These in the Planck length that described
sort of the smallest length that makes sense

00:11:20.120 --> 00:11:22.600
in a, in a quantum theory of gravity.

00:11:22.600 --> 00:11:26.960
Squares of the size 10 to the minus 33 centimeters
or so if you want to.

00:11:26.960 --> 00:11:28.180
Yeah.

00:11:28.180 --> 00:11:32.120
Each side of the square is so-
So you basically count the number of those

00:11:32.120 --> 00:11:39.010
little squares that are on the event horizon,
and that's the amount of entropy it has.

00:11:39.010 --> 00:11:44.860
So if we now took- If this is true, and we
were to take a particle that sort of carried

00:11:44.860 --> 00:11:49.930
one unit of entropy, what would we expect
to happen if this is true?

00:11:49.930 --> 00:11:53.030
We expect this area to grow exactly by one
square.

00:11:53.030 --> 00:11:54.650
So let's, let's sort of do that.

00:11:54.650 --> 00:11:59.820
We have- This is actually a real real film
of, of space here.

00:11:59.820 --> 00:12:04.290
So let's get our particle going in to this
guy over here.

00:12:04.290 --> 00:12:05.290
Good.

00:12:05.290 --> 00:12:08.331
So the one on the left was the original black
hole.

00:12:08.331 --> 00:12:12.140
The one on the right is the larger one after
particles fall in.

00:12:12.140 --> 00:12:18.300
So if we take all those little squares, and
we smear them out on the larger black hole,

00:12:18.300 --> 00:12:21.600
what we'd expect to happen, something like
that.

00:12:21.600 --> 00:12:22.600
Exactly.

00:12:22.600 --> 00:12:23.960
So one one more square.

00:12:23.960 --> 00:12:29.460
So we throw a sort of one particle in that
carries one unit of disorder, and the black

00:12:29.460 --> 00:12:31.350
hole eats it.

00:12:31.350 --> 00:12:36.170
It grows a little bit, and in fact you can
calculate that the amount by which it will

00:12:36.170 --> 00:12:40.910
expand can accommodate one more of these little
tiles.

00:12:40.910 --> 00:12:45.940
And that makes us a consistent way of thinking
about black balls.

00:12:45.940 --> 00:12:46.940
So.

00:12:46.940 --> 00:12:52.000
So this was like an important- How, I mean,
how important was, was this kind of an insight?

00:12:52.000 --> 00:12:56.420
Well, this was the amazing inside because
it was the first time where Einstein's theory

00:12:56.420 --> 00:12:59.710
of relativity was combined with quantum effects.

00:12:59.710 --> 00:13:05.750
And that is what Hawking used to actually
argue that the entropy of a black hole has

00:13:05.750 --> 00:13:07.730
something to do with the area.

00:13:07.730 --> 00:13:12.370
And so this combination of quantum theory
and Einstein's theory was quite novel, and

00:13:12.370 --> 00:13:15.490
this was in early seventies, mid sevens.

00:13:15.490 --> 00:13:20.740
So is this the end of the story when it comes
to entropy and black holes?

00:13:20.740 --> 00:13:23.950
Like what, what, what issues does this leave
over?

00:13:23.950 --> 00:13:29.650
Well actually the problem was that Hawking's
argument showed that there must be information,

00:13:29.650 --> 00:13:35.380
but on the other hand, the horizon of a black
hole is featureless, so you don't see these

00:13:35.380 --> 00:13:36.630
points, squares or anything.

00:13:36.630 --> 00:13:40.140
So there seems to be no room for information
to be stored on it.

00:13:40.140 --> 00:13:45.310
So in fact, if you use Einstein's equations
and solve it, you find there is exactly one

00:13:45.310 --> 00:13:48.640
solution for a spherical symmetric black hole.

00:13:48.640 --> 00:13:50.770
One solution means it has zero entropy.

00:13:50.770 --> 00:13:53.000
It's exactly ordered state.

00:13:53.000 --> 00:13:54.000
There's no choice.

00:13:54.000 --> 00:13:55.000
One piece of information.

00:13:55.000 --> 00:13:56.000
One piece of information.

00:13:56.000 --> 00:14:00.530
And that is inconsistent with what Hawking
found when he combined Einstein's theory of

00:14:00.530 --> 00:14:03.500
quantum mechanics, where he found a huge amount
of information.

00:14:03.500 --> 00:14:07.950
And the question is where is this huge amount
of information stored?

00:14:07.950 --> 00:14:15.040
Is it the shape of the horizon, or is it-
What aspect of black hole encodes the microscopic

00:14:15.040 --> 00:14:20.500
structure of the black hole that was open
when Hawking discovered this fact.

00:14:20.500 --> 00:14:25.500
So, so for Hawking's idea to work, let's just
go back to my little example of sort of throwing

00:14:25.500 --> 00:14:26.500
the wallet in.

00:14:26.500 --> 00:14:30.950
So, so in that little example I threw the
wallet and he said where did the information

00:14:30.950 --> 00:14:31.950
go?

00:14:31.950 --> 00:14:37.290
So, so the new idea, if it's true, would be
I take my wallet and I throw it in and rather

00:14:37.290 --> 00:14:43.550
than it disappearing, the idea roughly speaking
would be the information that is in my wallet,

00:14:43.550 --> 00:14:47.370
the configuration of all the atoms and molecules
that goes toward the black hole.

00:14:47.370 --> 00:14:53.750
And we want it to be the case that that information
kind of like smears itself out on the outside

00:14:53.750 --> 00:14:57.380
and is encoded in sort of bits, ones and zeros.

00:14:57.380 --> 00:14:58.380
That's the picture.

00:14:58.380 --> 00:14:59.380
That's the picture.

00:14:59.380 --> 00:15:04.370
And the principal were this true, I could
actually extract that information in principle

00:15:04.370 --> 00:15:08.520
and use it to rebuild anything that fell in.

00:15:08.520 --> 00:15:10.480
In this case it'd be my wallet.

00:15:10.480 --> 00:15:11.480
So that's the idea.

00:15:11.480 --> 00:15:14.150
That's if the information is not lost, that
information is there.

00:15:14.150 --> 00:15:15.380
That's what we would expect.

00:15:15.380 --> 00:15:16.380
Yes.

00:15:16.380 --> 00:15:17.380
That's the hole.

00:15:17.380 --> 00:15:18.670
And the puzzle is, is that actually-
Is that actually true?

00:15:18.670 --> 00:15:21.590
And how do you actually see this information
and where is it stored?

00:15:21.590 --> 00:15:26.240
Or how do you account for the microstates
of the black hole or the entropy of the black

00:15:26.240 --> 00:15:27.240
hole?

00:15:27.240 --> 00:15:28.310
And that's where you come in.

00:15:28.310 --> 00:15:33.390
Well me and my colleagues and string theory
began studying these questions more seriously

00:15:33.390 --> 00:15:37.210
with the advent of duality, some interests
in string theory.

00:15:37.210 --> 00:15:39.690
So firstly-
Can you say a few words about string theory?

00:15:39.690 --> 00:15:41.570
I should say a few words about string theory.

00:15:41.570 --> 00:15:49.210
So string theory is, is a framework where
we believe combines Einstein's theory of relativity

00:15:49.210 --> 00:15:51.780
with quantum theory in a consistent framework.

00:15:51.780 --> 00:15:57.850
And as part of it, it demands that the entities
making up the matter is not, are not just

00:15:57.850 --> 00:16:04.090
point particles, but entities like strings
or membranes or extended objects have to play

00:16:04.090 --> 00:16:05.570
a key role.

00:16:05.570 --> 00:16:07.060
And so that's one aspect of string theory.

00:16:07.060 --> 00:16:10.760
It is a little, if you want to take us through-
This is just a little example, what string

00:16:10.760 --> 00:16:11.980
theory might look like.

00:16:11.980 --> 00:16:15.530
So you've got a piece of matter, and we're
going to dive into it.

00:16:15.530 --> 00:16:18.240
And then you're saying that if it goes sufficiently
deep-

00:16:18.240 --> 00:16:21.900
So if you go to the beginning, inside, you'll
see the atoms first.

00:16:21.900 --> 00:16:24.770
And uh, you know, inside the atoms you see
the nucleus.

00:16:24.770 --> 00:16:30.110
And the nucleus you see here, like it's the
protons and neutrons, I'm assuming.

00:16:30.110 --> 00:16:35.510
Then you go inside them, you see the quarks,
and once you see the quark you can go inside

00:16:35.510 --> 00:16:39.830
them, and if you zoom in further you might
see actually that these featureless quarks

00:16:39.830 --> 00:16:43.910
that you think are point particle actually
are tiny strings, vibrating strings of some

00:16:43.910 --> 00:16:48.930
sort, and that's the kind of a picture that
we expect to be true in the context of string

00:16:48.930 --> 00:16:49.930
theory.

00:16:49.930 --> 00:16:53.150
That point particles are point-like because
we are not zoomed in sufficiently close to

00:16:53.150 --> 00:16:56.470
them, and if you zoom in enough, they will
have features like strings.

00:16:56.470 --> 00:16:59.360
How big would those-
We don't know exactly, but it could be like

00:16:59.360 --> 00:17:03.340
10 to the minus 30 centimeter- It's somewhat
close to the Planck scale that we discussed

00:17:03.340 --> 00:17:07.610
already in the context of the areas of the
horizon of the black hole.

00:17:07.610 --> 00:17:12.000
So that's the basic feature of string theory,
but as one of the aspects of string theory

00:17:12.000 --> 00:17:18.060
that was originally one of the negative features,
was that string theory predicted that the

00:17:18.060 --> 00:17:24.430
spatial dimension of the universe is not three,
and that was manifested in contradiction with

00:17:24.430 --> 00:17:26.480
the fact that we think we live in three spatial
dimensions.

00:17:26.480 --> 00:17:28.800
We have three spatial dimensions.

00:17:28.800 --> 00:17:33.250
String theory seem to demand that we have
nine spatial dimensions, and so this was a

00:17:33.250 --> 00:17:37.450
contradiction originally, and physicists thought
how could this possibly work?

00:17:37.450 --> 00:17:43.790
And one resolution was, oh, these extra- six
extra dimensions that we have are so tiny,

00:17:43.790 --> 00:17:46.300
like little circles at every point in space.

00:17:46.300 --> 00:17:51.200
Let's imagine six dimensions, six circles
at each point, six types of circles which

00:17:51.200 --> 00:17:53.580
are so tiny that you cannot see.

00:17:53.580 --> 00:17:57.040
So the idea would be the macroscopic dimensions
are only three.

00:17:57.040 --> 00:18:01.290
The big ones are three, but the tiny ones
are six.

00:18:01.290 --> 00:18:06.130
And the tiny ones are hard to see unless you
really zoom in and that's difficult to do

00:18:06.130 --> 00:18:09.530
with the energies that are available to us
in our experiments today.

00:18:09.530 --> 00:18:10.530
But the idea would be there.

00:18:10.530 --> 00:18:13.540
You've got these, these big dimensions that
we know about.

00:18:13.540 --> 00:18:19.500
These extra dimensions, and then how do you
relate this to this idea of black hole?

00:18:19.500 --> 00:18:22.140
this was actually a negative feature for string
theory.

00:18:22.140 --> 00:18:23.570
So string theorists were kind of struggling.

00:18:23.570 --> 00:18:25.190
Yeah, we have these six extra dimension.

00:18:25.190 --> 00:18:27.280
It's a little embarrassing.

00:18:27.280 --> 00:18:29.040
It's tiny.

00:18:29.040 --> 00:18:31.350
You don't see them.

00:18:31.350 --> 00:18:35.820
And then other people said, sure, sure, yes,
yes.

00:18:35.820 --> 00:18:40.610
So this was the state of the art to mid-nineties
where we didn't know what to do with these

00:18:40.610 --> 00:18:41.610
extra dimensions.

00:18:41.610 --> 00:18:46.140
They were there just to make the theory consistent
and we were hiding it away in tiny little

00:18:46.140 --> 00:18:47.140
things.

00:18:47.140 --> 00:18:50.910
And then came the question about the black
holes and the black hole had the opposite

00:18:50.910 --> 00:18:51.910
problem.

00:18:51.910 --> 00:18:56.050
The problem with the black hole was we were
missing information of the black hole.

00:18:56.050 --> 00:18:57.830
So where were they?

00:18:57.830 --> 00:18:59.600
What were that information?

00:18:59.600 --> 00:19:02.920
It turned out that these two problems canceled
each other out.

00:19:02.920 --> 00:19:06.140
And so this- the problem that was where are
they in-?

00:19:06.140 --> 00:19:12.040
Where are the ingredients or the degrees of
freedom that constitutes black hole ended

00:19:12.040 --> 00:19:17.280
up in the internal spatial, tiny spacial things
that I was talking about.

00:19:17.280 --> 00:19:24.280
So as you can see here, the red, donut shape
object, there is to represent these tiny internal

00:19:24.280 --> 00:19:26.290
dimensions.

00:19:26.290 --> 00:19:32.510
And the blue sheet that you see represents
the macroscopic three macroscopic dimensions.

00:19:32.510 --> 00:19:38.400
And so the idea there is that if you take
a string or a membrane or one of these extended

00:19:38.400 --> 00:19:45.850
objects and wrap it around the cycles on these
external tiny spaces like a torus or like

00:19:45.850 --> 00:19:51.810
a circle or donut or whatnot shape you have,
it will create a mass at the point where you're

00:19:51.810 --> 00:19:56.330
wrapping around that circle, and the more
it wraps around, the more mass is going to

00:19:56.330 --> 00:20:01.540
be constituting there, and the more it's gonna
shape and distort the geometry of space around

00:20:01.540 --> 00:20:02.540
it.

00:20:02.540 --> 00:20:06.780
And if that is enough of it, it's going to
create this warped space, which is what we

00:20:06.780 --> 00:20:08.430
call the black hole.

00:20:08.430 --> 00:20:14.060
So the black hole itself can be viewed as
these strings or membranes wrapped around

00:20:14.060 --> 00:20:17.390
these extra cycles of this tiny space.

00:20:17.390 --> 00:20:24.150
And so then if the question is what accounts
for the degrees of freedom of the black hole

00:20:24.150 --> 00:20:29.030
it are, these are precisely the strings or
membranes wrapped around these extra dimensions.

00:20:29.030 --> 00:20:30.710
How many are there?

00:20:30.710 --> 00:20:32.300
How many degrees of freedom are there?

00:20:32.300 --> 00:20:33.760
What are the entropy?

00:20:33.760 --> 00:20:39.830
Gets translated to how many ways these extra
strings or membranes can wrap around these

00:20:39.830 --> 00:20:41.190
extra dimensions.

00:20:41.190 --> 00:20:45.810
And there are many ways and that accounted
for the entropy of the black hole.

00:20:45.810 --> 00:20:51.100
So with Andy- Andy Strominger and I did actually
a computation of how much entropy is there

00:20:51.100 --> 00:20:56.080
in these wrappings of these string around
the external, around these internal dimensions

00:20:56.080 --> 00:20:57.080
of string theory.

00:20:57.080 --> 00:21:02.550
And we found exact match with a prediction
that Beckenstein and Hawking had made about

00:21:02.550 --> 00:21:04.370
the entropy of these black holes.

00:21:04.370 --> 00:21:06.380
So just to quickly unpack a little bit.

00:21:06.380 --> 00:21:12.930
So, so basically you found a new way of describing
black holes in string theory, which makes

00:21:12.930 --> 00:21:15.050
use of the extra curled up dimensions.

00:21:15.050 --> 00:21:18.400
The red part of this image that we find here.

00:21:18.400 --> 00:21:23.490
And you're saying that by wrapping these strings
or ingredients of string theory around the

00:21:23.490 --> 00:21:29.080
extra dimensions, you can create a warping
in space that looks just like the black hole

00:21:29.080 --> 00:21:32.740
that we've been talking about for many years
before string theory even was on anybody's

00:21:32.740 --> 00:21:33.740
mind.

00:21:33.740 --> 00:21:39.700
But in this description you can do a direct
count of the amount of information necessary

00:21:39.700 --> 00:21:45.000
to describe the situation and bang on, it
matches what Hawking said.

00:21:45.000 --> 00:21:46.000
Yes.

00:21:46.000 --> 00:21:53.340
So, so this gives us insight into one possible
way of describing the internal structure of

00:21:53.340 --> 00:21:58.440
a black hole, which in many ways I think they,
the community views as one of the most important

00:21:58.440 --> 00:22:01.620
pieces of data that string theory may be going
in the right direction.

00:22:01.620 --> 00:22:05.890
We don't have experimental support for these
ideas, but here's a mathematical piece of

00:22:05.890 --> 00:22:10.230
experimental support, if you will, that it
matches ideas that had been on the table for

00:22:10.230 --> 00:22:11.820
25, 30 years.

00:22:11.820 --> 00:22:12.820
So.

00:22:12.820 --> 00:22:19.990
So this is a key step forward, but now let's
turn to some of the puzzles that still remain

00:22:19.990 --> 00:22:26.300
and part of the puzzles surround something
that's tightly related to what we've been

00:22:26.300 --> 00:22:29.800
talking about, which is something called Hawking
radiation.

00:22:29.800 --> 00:22:32.770
Can you just tell us a bit about what Hawking
radiation is?

00:22:32.770 --> 00:22:38.790
So Hawking after discovering the properties
of having information in the black and the

00:22:38.790 --> 00:22:44.940
entropy, he also noticed that the black hose
actually are not quite black.

00:22:44.940 --> 00:22:49.770
And what happens if you go near the event
horizon of the black hole.

00:22:49.770 --> 00:22:54.360
What happens is that you get pairs of particles
created out of vacuum.

00:22:54.360 --> 00:22:58.960
These are quantum fluctuations, the quantum
fluctuations always creating particles and

00:22:58.960 --> 00:23:03.770
antiparticles in pairs, and typically they
go in and out without doing anything, but

00:23:03.770 --> 00:23:08.050
if they are near the event horizon, one of
them per chance could go towards the inside

00:23:08.050 --> 00:23:11.320
of the black hole and then one of them could
go outside.

00:23:11.320 --> 00:23:15.630
And the moment she goes inside has no way
of getting out, so stuck there, but the one

00:23:15.630 --> 00:23:21.100
going out so can leave and go far away from
it all the way to infinity, and that looks

00:23:21.100 --> 00:23:24.890
like a radiation from the viewpoint of somebody
outside.

00:23:24.890 --> 00:23:29.780
So in other words, from the viewpoint of somebody
outside, the black hole is actually radiating

00:23:29.780 --> 00:23:31.030
energy.

00:23:31.030 --> 00:23:37.190
As it radiates energy, it loses mass, and
it shrinks and shrinks and shrinks, until

00:23:37.190 --> 00:23:45.870
after a while the black hole totally disappears
where there's no more energy left to, to emit.

00:23:45.870 --> 00:23:54.600
So once the, once the black hole disappears,
the question is what happens to all that information

00:23:54.600 --> 00:23:56.610
that went into the black hole?

00:23:56.610 --> 00:23:59.130
What happened to that area was talking about?

00:23:59.130 --> 00:24:00.980
Where is that information gone?

00:24:00.980 --> 00:24:06.990
So this was a question that Hawking basically
posed, perhaps you recall the discussion between

00:24:06.990 --> 00:24:13.520
Einstein and Bohr that uh, Einstein did not
like the probabilistic aspect of quantum mechanics.

00:24:13.520 --> 00:24:18.130
And so he said, God does not play dice.

00:24:18.130 --> 00:24:26.420
And Niels Bohr responded by saying to Einstein,
stopped telling God what to do, and uh, Hawking

00:24:26.420 --> 00:24:28.160
added his own wrinkle on this.

00:24:28.160 --> 00:24:33.500
He said, well, what happens if you throw a
dice inside the black hole, and the black

00:24:33.500 --> 00:24:36.540
hole after a while disappears and with it
disappears the dice?

00:24:36.540 --> 00:24:41.750
So, and then he said, not only does God throw
dice, he sometimes throws it, throws them

00:24:41.750 --> 00:24:43.220
where they cannot be seen.

00:24:43.220 --> 00:24:47.630
And then you made a little little cake for
him.

00:24:47.630 --> 00:24:52.470
mWe had the lucky occasion of having a Stephen
Hawking as our guests at his home a couple

00:24:52.470 --> 00:24:56.350
of years ago when he was visiting Harvard,
and then we made the cake in the form of a

00:24:56.350 --> 00:25:00.480
black hole with a dice.

00:25:00.480 --> 00:25:05.080
And the book, his famous book, which is actually
made of- It's a cake and it's actually gluten

00:25:05.080 --> 00:25:06.670
free because he was allergic.

00:25:06.670 --> 00:25:14.010
So, so, so Stephen had his cake and he could
eat it too.

00:25:14.010 --> 00:25:16.270
So yeah.

00:25:16.270 --> 00:25:21.440
So in that, in that picture, you see that
there are two, two dice hanging there floating

00:25:21.440 --> 00:25:26.140
towards the black hole, and it's going in
and that's the, the issue of the information

00:25:26.140 --> 00:25:27.140
puzzle.

00:25:27.140 --> 00:25:31.570
You lose the information of what, what is
the dice roll, and uh, that's what's called

00:25:31.570 --> 00:25:37.010
the information puzzle because if the dice
rolls some number and the black hole evaporates,

00:25:37.010 --> 00:25:38.200
it disappears.

00:25:38.200 --> 00:25:42.370
With it disappears the information about what
the role of the dice was, and that's called

00:25:42.370 --> 00:25:46.500
the information posture, which we still are
struggling to understand even today.

00:25:46.500 --> 00:25:53.590
Now, most people in the field for a long time
have anticipated that somehow the information

00:25:53.590 --> 00:25:58.770
does get out with that radiation that we saw
coming off of the black hole.

00:25:58.770 --> 00:26:03.260
Now for a while, Stephen Hawking said that
the information would, would not command-

00:26:03.260 --> 00:26:05.040
And that quote was quite serious.

00:26:05.040 --> 00:26:09.630
He was saying that we have to rethink quantum
mechanics because the information does not

00:26:09.630 --> 00:26:11.080
come back out.

00:26:11.080 --> 00:26:13.860
He then changed his mind on that.

00:26:13.860 --> 00:26:14.860
Yes.

00:26:14.860 --> 00:26:18.310
So part of the reason for this is the discovery
of a more detailed version of what is called

00:26:18.310 --> 00:26:19.420
holography.

00:26:19.420 --> 00:26:25.770
In other words, this, this began with the
work already back in the, in the long time

00:26:25.770 --> 00:26:30.380
ago, but became more precise with the work
of one mother center where it was discovered

00:26:30.380 --> 00:26:35.860
how that you can describe a gravitational
system precisely using objects which are much

00:26:35.860 --> 00:26:37.130
simpler to understand.

00:26:37.130 --> 00:26:41.809
And in particular in that system you could
prove mathematically rigorously, there's no

00:26:41.809 --> 00:26:43.940
way you can lose information.

00:26:43.940 --> 00:26:49.000
So therefore, indirectly there was a proof
that whatever happens to black holes, the

00:26:49.000 --> 00:26:50.950
information should get out.

00:26:50.950 --> 00:26:56.680
So that part was kind of beginning to be established
using these kinds of arguments and string

00:26:56.680 --> 00:26:58.570
theory.

00:26:58.570 --> 00:27:03.800
But the wrinkle is that even though there's
this mathematical proof that this should happen,

00:27:03.800 --> 00:27:08.120
we still did not know exactly how, and some
people thought maybe the information gets

00:27:08.120 --> 00:27:13.240
out with this radiation, as you were saying,
Brian, very gradually and very tiny bit at

00:27:13.240 --> 00:27:16.170
a time, but gradually builds up to give you
information out.

00:27:16.170 --> 00:27:22.170
And this is what, uh, in the early 2000s,
I believe, Stephen agreed that this is the

00:27:22.170 --> 00:27:23.170
resolution.

00:27:23.170 --> 00:27:27.040
But I still think, and I think the community
still believes we haven't really understood

00:27:27.040 --> 00:27:28.620
how this happens exactly.

00:27:28.620 --> 00:27:35.470
So the information puzzle about how this information
comes out is still one of the big mysteries

00:27:35.470 --> 00:27:37.780
of black holes, and we don't really understand
it.

00:27:37.780 --> 00:27:42.340
So do you think it's possible that we will
one day conclude that the original view of

00:27:42.340 --> 00:27:45.090
Hawking was right, that the information does
not get at?

00:27:45.090 --> 00:27:46.840
Is that still on the table in your view?

00:27:46.840 --> 00:27:51.420
Well, I think it sounds much less likely given
that we think we understand a dual description

00:27:51.420 --> 00:27:53.630
and other description of these black holes.

00:27:53.630 --> 00:27:58.550
So it seems like that seems much less likely
now, but you know, we can never say we know

00:27:58.550 --> 00:27:59.990
everything about the subject.

00:27:59.990 --> 00:28:01.110
That's our current understanding.

00:28:01.110 --> 00:28:06.420
It could of course evolve, but even even with
that understanding, it's not- Our understanding

00:28:06.420 --> 00:28:10.170
is not sufficiently detailed to convince us
of this, of how it happens.

00:28:10.170 --> 00:28:15.900
Now a lot of our discussion here has been
focusing on what happens at the edge of a

00:28:15.900 --> 00:28:20.240
black hole, at the event horizon to a black
hole that it may be where the information

00:28:20.240 --> 00:28:22.880
is stored, that may be where the entropy is
stored.

00:28:22.880 --> 00:28:26.800
We haven't said much about what happens at
the center.

00:28:26.800 --> 00:28:29.150
Can we just spend a little time talking about
that?

00:28:29.150 --> 00:28:34.250
Well, one of the things that happens in the
center of the black hole is that there's this

00:28:34.250 --> 00:28:39.900
singularity, this infinity that Brian discussed
already, this division by zero analogy, and

00:28:39.900 --> 00:28:41.250
we don't believe that's physical.

00:28:41.250 --> 00:28:43.550
We believe that's just the phantom of our
equation.

00:28:43.550 --> 00:28:48.300
It's just the mathematical thing that comes
out and that means that our description breaks

00:28:48.300 --> 00:28:49.300
down.

00:28:49.300 --> 00:28:50.300
Not that there is an infinity.

00:28:50.300 --> 00:28:55.130
Now quickly, the infinity could be physically
that you've got all this mass crushed to zero

00:28:55.130 --> 00:28:56.130
size.

00:28:56.130 --> 00:28:57.130
Zero size is not physical.

00:28:57.130 --> 00:29:01.700
Yes for us, there nothing smaller than Planck's
length, which is about 10 to the minus 33

00:29:01.700 --> 00:29:02.700
centimeters.

00:29:02.700 --> 00:29:06.840
The notion of space breaks down anything smaller
than that, so there cannot be an infinity

00:29:06.840 --> 00:29:11.620
in physics, we don't believe so, so we don't
understand what is happening at that, at that

00:29:11.620 --> 00:29:12.670
in that way.

00:29:12.670 --> 00:29:17.830
And the understanding of this singularity,
we are not at all close to understanding what's

00:29:17.830 --> 00:29:19.990
happening there.

00:29:19.990 --> 00:29:23.130
We know that there should be a good description,
but we don't have it.

00:29:23.130 --> 00:29:28.450
And why that's important is that, well, we
want to know what's happening inside the black

00:29:28.450 --> 00:29:29.450
holes.

00:29:29.450 --> 00:29:33.730
Of course we can send somebody and find out
what happens to that infinity, but that would

00:29:33.730 --> 00:29:35.150
not be a fair thing.

00:29:35.150 --> 00:29:39.330
And even if we did that, even if we said,
well, we send some creature not us, the robot

00:29:39.330 --> 00:29:44.670
or something, the robot cannot come back to
tell us what happened or what they saw.

00:29:44.670 --> 00:29:47.350
So because nothing can escape from black holes.

00:29:47.350 --> 00:29:48.350
So it won't be useful.

00:29:48.350 --> 00:29:51.400
So we have to figure it out without going
inside the black hole, what's going on there,

00:29:51.400 --> 00:29:56.490
and that's still, we have no, uh, not only
you don't have observational data, we don't

00:29:56.490 --> 00:29:58.580
have enough theoretical understanding.

00:29:58.580 --> 00:30:03.100
And we think it's important not just for the
black holes, but it turns out that those infinities

00:30:03.100 --> 00:30:08.640
that we see, if you try to pass through those
infinities to the other side, so to speak,

00:30:08.640 --> 00:30:14.390
it turns out it looks very much like the infinities
we see at the beginning of our own universe.

00:30:14.390 --> 00:30:18.059
It is as if our universe is emerging from
inside of one of these black holes.

00:30:18.059 --> 00:30:19.200
Along those lines.

00:30:19.200 --> 00:30:20.990
And don't lose your train of thought there.

00:30:20.990 --> 00:30:24.330
Oftentimes people think of the center of the
black hole as a location in space.

00:30:24.330 --> 00:30:25.330
The center.

00:30:25.330 --> 00:30:28.140
Is that the right way of-
No, actually it's like in a given time, it

00:30:28.140 --> 00:30:32.360
happens at a given time and that's because
the nature of space and time changes as you

00:30:32.360 --> 00:30:34.870
enter the black hole.

00:30:34.870 --> 00:30:36.880
When you cross the event horizon of the black
hole.

00:30:36.880 --> 00:30:41.800
So one of the reasons why you can't get out
is because you can't stop time from ticking

00:30:41.800 --> 00:30:42.800
forward.

00:30:42.800 --> 00:30:43.800
Exactly.

00:30:43.800 --> 00:30:46.020
So going forward and forward in time is like
going closer and closer to the singularity-

00:30:46.020 --> 00:30:48.200
And then at certain time you hit the singularity.

00:30:48.200 --> 00:30:49.200
That's the time.

00:30:49.200 --> 00:30:52.480
It's at the given time is that-
So the singularity could be the end of time

00:30:52.480 --> 00:30:53.830
in some sense.

00:30:53.830 --> 00:30:55.510
It could be, but we don't believe that.

00:30:55.510 --> 00:31:00.140
And then what you would say what's the after
that, and that looks very much like what could

00:31:00.140 --> 00:31:02.760
be a new universe or something.

00:31:02.760 --> 00:31:06.580
So we are interested in understanding the
resolution to the issue of singularity of

00:31:06.580 --> 00:31:10.080
black holes, and I think it's very exciting.

00:31:10.080 --> 00:31:14.740
We know that black holes, as we discussed
already in this program, there are huge amount

00:31:14.740 --> 00:31:16.600
of evidence that they exist or they are there.

00:31:16.600 --> 00:31:17.600
We cannot say.

00:31:17.600 --> 00:31:18.660
We're just imagining.

00:31:18.660 --> 00:31:19.660
So what is that?

00:31:19.660 --> 00:31:20.660
What is a singularity?

00:31:20.660 --> 00:31:21.660
What are we learning from it?

00:31:21.660 --> 00:31:26.420
This one of the most beautiful, mysterious
objects I think in the universe, and hopefully,

00:31:26.420 --> 00:31:30.450
by some experiments and maybe by some theoretical
understanding, we will have some progress

00:31:30.450 --> 00:31:31.450
in that.

00:31:31.450 --> 00:31:32.450
So what's your guess?

00:31:32.450 --> 00:31:39.270
I mean if we come back and we invite you back
for the, I dunno, the 2025 World Science Festival,

00:31:39.270 --> 00:31:42.770
can you give us the answer to what happens
at the center of a black hole?

00:31:42.770 --> 00:31:43.950
Maybe invite in 2050 if I'm alive.

00:31:43.950 --> 00:31:44.950
2050.

00:31:44.950 --> 00:31:45.950
All right.

00:31:45.950 --> 00:31:46.950
Good.

00:31:46.950 --> 00:31:47.950
A good prediction.

00:31:47.950 --> 00:31:49.820
A safe prediction.

00:31:49.820 --> 00:31:52.370
Please join me in thanking Cumrun Vafa.

00:31:52.370 --> 00:31:53.430
Thank you.

00:31:53.430 --> 00:31:55.540
Thank you very much.

00:31:55.540 --> 00:31:56.540
